<Project>

  <!--
    ==============================================================================================
    Directory.Packages.props
    The single authoritative package version manifest for the PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition.
    ==============================================================================================

    WHAT THIS FILE IS
    One PackageVersion entry per package used anywhere in this repository. Every project file, the
    six shared libraries, their six sibling test projects, the four service applications and the
    four service test projects alike, therefore declares VERSIONLESS PackageReference entries and
    inherits its versions from here by MSBuild directory walk up.

    This file is INERT on its own. It takes effect only because Directory.Build.props, its sibling
    at this same repository root, sets ManagePackageVersionsCentrally to true.

    WHY CENTRAL PACKAGE MANAGEMENT IS MANDATORY HERE, NOT MERELY CONVENIENT
    It is the mechanism that forces the two mandatory security pins below onto all four services
    from exactly one place. Without it, a single service could restore a vulnerable transitive
    package while the dependency audit still passed everywhere else, and nothing would report it.

    EXACTLY ONE COPY OF THIS FILE MAY EXIST
    It lives at the repository root so that a service under services/<service-name>/ inherits these
    pins by walk up while still restoring, building and testing independently from a clean
    checkout. Do NOT create a per service copy. A second copy reintroduces precisely the drift this
    file exists to prevent, and it would do so silently.

    PROVENANCE: THERE IS NO LEGACY SOURCE EQUIVALENT AND NO VERSION TO MIGRATE
    The repository this .NET tree is being added to is an Appeon PowerBuilder 2021 framework. It
    contains zero *.csproj, zero packages.config, zero package.json and no lock file of any kind,
    so every entry below is an addition. There are no dependency updates and no dependency
    removals in this refactor. The only version facts the repository asserts about itself are
    legacy ones, and none of them constrains anything here:

        appruntimeversion "21.0.0.1311"   ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/pfw.sra
        DataWindow release 12.5           ws_objects/pfw.tests.pbl.src/dw_sqlite.srd
        framework 3.0.7.2062              logfile.md

    The two PowerBuilder project objects, ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/project.srj and
    ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/p_pfw.srj, are reference only, and only for build INTENT: they disagree
    on library count (29 versus 28) and on vendor (Appeon versus Sybase), and BOTH name
    pfw.utility.imgcodec.pbl, which does not exist anywhere in the repository, so neither would
    build as written. The legacy tree is read only and is the behavioural oracle for parity
    testing. Nothing in this file references it, and nothing in this file may.

    HOW EVERY VERSION BELOW WAS ESTABLISHED
    Each was verified twice. First it was resolved as a real published STABLE release from the
    NuGet flat container index. Then all of them together were assembled into a single net10.0
    project and COMPILED with SDK 10.0.302 under TreatWarningsAsErrors and central package
    management. The composite restored clean with no NuGet audit warnings and reported
    "Build succeeded, 0 Warning(s), 0 Error(s)", and a protocol definition carrying
    GrpcServices="Both" genuinely generated both its message and its service stubs. No version
    here is a guess, a placeholder, or "latest". Do not change any of them.

    REGISTRY
    nuget.org for every entry. There are no private or internal package feeds in this refactor,
    and consequently no credential, key, token, password or feed URL appears anywhere in this file.
    The repository's one npm dependency, @playwright/test 1.62.1, belongs to
    tests/e2e/package.json and deliberately has no representation here.

    TRANSITIVE VERSIONS THAT WILL APPEAR IN LOCK FILES AND DIFFER FROM THE DIRECT PINS
    Recorded so that a reader does not mistake any of them for drift. None needs its own entry.

      Google.Protobuf resolves to 3.31.1, NOT the newer published line, because Grpc.AspNetCore
        2.83.0 constrains it. Grpc.Tools arrives at 2.83.0 alongside it.
      The Polly family resolves to 8.4.2, specifically Polly.Core, Polly.Extensions and
        Polly.RateLimiting, pulled in by Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience together with
        Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience 10.8.0.
      The identity model graph deliberately MIXES two versions, and this is expected rather than
        drift. Observed resolution:
          at 8.22.0, unified upward by the direct token minting reference:
            Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens, Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens,
            Microsoft.IdentityModel.Abstractions, Microsoft.IdentityModel.Logging
          at 8.19.2, as pulled by the JWT bearer handler:
            Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols, Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect,
            System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt
        That combination was verified compatible with zero warnings and no vulnerable packages. Do
        NOT try to unify it by pinning Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens or any of the 8.19.2
        packages: no project references them directly, so such an entry would pin something nobody
        asked for and would drift the moment either direct reference moves.

    HOUSE RULES FOR EDITING THIS FILE
      Exact three part versions only. No floating version, no wildcard, no range, and no
        prerelease, preview, rc or beta suffix.
      No VersionOverride, here or in any project file. An override in a project defeats the
        central pin and is exactly how one service drifts away from the others.
      No PackageReference elements. This file declares VERSIONS; projects declare REFERENCES.
      No entry for a package used only by a deferred capability area. DesignSystem, Documents,
        Integration and ScriptBridge receive no code in this phase, so they receive no dependency.
      A double hyphen is not legal inside an XML comment, so the command line switches quoted in
        the comments below are written without their leading dashes. Restore them when running.

    HOW THE TWO HALVES OF THE CONTRACT ARE ENFORCED, BOTH VERIFIED ON THIS REPOSITORY
      If a project declares a Version on a PackageReference, restore FAILS with NU1008: "The
        following PackageReference items cannot define a value for Version". So a versionless
        reference is not a convention to remember, it is a build requirement.
      If a project references a package that has no PackageVersion entry HERE, restore FAILS with
        NU1010: "The following PackageReference items do not define a corresponding PackageVersion
        item". So adding a package means adding it to this file, in the same change.
      Both arrive as errors rather than warnings, so neither can be merged past CI unnoticed.

    A trailing block at the end of this file records the packages that were DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED,
    together with the reason each was rejected, so that a future maintainer reads a decision
    instead of rediscovering it.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Restated here for self documentation. Directory.Build.props is what actually switches it
         on for the whole tree; this file is inert if that property is ever turned off. -->
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ============================================================================================
       gRPC AND CONTRACTS
       Consumed by PowerFramework.Contracts, DataServices and Persistence (server sides), and by
       the Gateway and DataServices client sides. gRPC is the chosen transport for the internal
       east west edges because those interfaces are ordered, strongly typed and streaming shaped.
       ============================================================================================ -->
  <ItemGroup Label="gRPC and contracts">
    <!-- gRPC server, client, and protocol definition code generation in one package.
         It pulls Grpc.Tools 2.83.0 and Google.Protobuf 3.31.1 TRANSITIVELY, so neither of those
         needs an entry of its own here. Verified by compilation: a .proto carrying
         GrpcServices="Both" genuinely generated its message types and its service base classes.
         Grpc.AspNetCore also brings Grpc.AspNetCore.Server.ClientFactory, so the two client sides
         need no additional package either. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Grpc.AspNetCore" Version="2.83.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ============================================================================================
       OPENAPI AND REST
       Consumed by Gateway (the sole ingress and composition root), Security, and the thin REST
       projection of DataServices that only Gateway calls.
       ============================================================================================ -->
  <ItemGroup Label="OpenAPI and REST">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" Version="10.0.10" />

    <!--
      MANDATORY PIN. Do not bump this, in EITHER direction. Both halves of the reason matter, and
      each half rules out one of the two obvious "helpful" edits:

      (a) It must not be left to transitive resolution, and must not be lower.
          Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0.10 pulls Microsoft.OpenApi 2.0.0 transitively, and the
          stock net10.0 web template therefore emits NU1903 at restore, a KNOWN HIGH SEVERITY
          advisory. An explicit direct pin is what overrides that and clears the audit.
          Reproduced on this repository by omitting the pin. Verbatim:
            error NU1903: Warning As Error: Package 'Microsoft.OpenApi' 2.0.0 has a known high
            severity vulnerability, https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v5pm-xwqc-g5wc
          Note that it arrives as an ERROR, not a warning, because Directory.Build.props sets
          TreatWarningsAsErrors, so removing this pin fails the restore outright.

      (b) It must not be higher either, because moving forward to the current major line BREAKS
          THE BUILD. Version 3.9.0 was tested directly and produces exactly two CS0200
          diagnostics. Verbatim, at lines 399 and 461 of the generated file:
            error CS0200: Property or indexer 'IOpenApiMediaType.Example' cannot be assigned to
            (it is read only)
          Critically, they are raised inside the SDK's OWN generated file,
            obj/<config>/net10.0/Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi.SourceGenerators/
            Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi.SourceGenerators.XmlCommentGenerator/
            OpenApiXmlCommentSupport.generated.cs
          rather than in any code of ours, so there is nothing to work around at the call site and
          no amount of editing our own sources will clear it. The cause is that the 10.0.10 OpenAPI
          XML comment source generator is compiled against the 2.x object model, in which the
          media type Example property is settable, and cannot be satisfied by a 3.x assembly, in
          which IOpenApiMediaType exposes it as read only.

      2.11.0 is the highest published 2.x, and is therefore the only value that is simultaneously
      non vulnerable AND compatible. Every REST service in this solution must respect it.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.OpenApi" Version="2.11.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ============================================================================================
       AUTHENTICATION AND TOKENS
       Decomposition creates this system's first ever network ingress, so every new boundary is
       authenticated from the outset. Security is the SOLE token issuer; the other three services
       hold verification material only and validate against Security's published JWKS.
       ============================================================================================ -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Authentication and tokens">
    <!-- Inbound JWT validation on /v1/ping and on every internal edge. Consumed by ALL FOUR
         services. The stock bearer handler fetches Security's /.well-known/jwks.json and OIDC
         discovery document with zero bespoke code, which is the reason Security speaks REST
         rather than gRPC: it keeps the security critical path in framework code. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="10.0.10" />

    <!-- Token MINTING, and consumed by the SECURITY SERVICE ONLY, because Security is the sole
         issuer in the system. No other service may reference this package: acquiring a minting
         dependency elsewhere would create a second signing authority, which the architecture
         forbids. Exactly one signing secret exists system wide, injected by configuration. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens" Version="8.22.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ============================================================================================
       DATA AND SQLITE
       Consumed by the Persistence service only, which is the sole service that generates or
       executes SQL and the sole holder of a storage provider.

       SQLITE IS THE ONLY DATABASE PROVIDER PINNED IN THIS REPOSITORY. That is a deliberate scope
       boundary, not an omission. SQLite is the only storage engine with evidence behind it in the
       legacy tree, which supplies exactly one connection URI and exactly one CREATE TABLE
       statement. Do NOT add Microsoft.Data.SqlClient, any Oracle client, Npgsql, or any other
       provider. The legacy SQL Server and Oracle behaviours this migration preserves are PAGING
       REWRITERS: pure string in, string out transforms whose acceptance criterion is byte exact
       generated SQL. They are therefore fully unit testable with no instance of either engine
       running, no client package is required for them, and adding one would fabricate a database.
       ============================================================================================ -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Data and SQLite">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="10.0.10" />

    <!-- Design time migration generation for the COMPANY schema, which is the only DDL anywhere in
         the repository. Reference it with PrivateAssets="all" from the project that owns the
         migrations, since it is a build time only concern. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.10" />

    <!-- ADO level access, required IN ADDITION to the EF Core provider rather than instead of it.
         Two legacy behaviours cannot be expressed through EF Core alone: the connection URI
         grammar of the form file?mode=rwc with its optional password, integrity check and journal
         mode extensions, and raw command execution with positional parameter binding. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.10" />

    <!--
      MANDATORY PIN. Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite 10.0.10 transitively pulls a 2.1.x
      SQLitePCLRaw native bundle, which raises NU1903 at restore. An explicit DIRECT reference to
      the 3.0.5 bundle overrides that transitive choice and clears the audit.
      Reproduced on this repository by omitting the pin. Verbatim:
        error NU1903: Warning As Error: Package 'SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3' 2.1.11 has a known
        high severity vulnerability, https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q
      Note which package is flagged: the advisory is against the NATIVE lib package that sits
      beneath the bundle, not against the bundle itself, which is why bumping the bundle is the
      correct remedy and why searching the advisory for the bundle name finds nothing.

      The resolved graph was inspected afterwards to confirm the fix is coherent rather than
      partial. All four SQLitePCLRaw packages land at 3.0.5 together:
        SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3, SQLitePCLRaw.core, SQLitePCLRaw.config.e_sqlite3 and
        SQLitePCLRaw.provider.e_sqlite3
      so the managed and native halves stay matched and no mismatched pair can load at runtime.

      With this pin and the Microsoft.OpenApi pin above BOTH in place, restore reports no audit
      warnings at all and the build reports 0 Warning(s), 0 Error(s). If NU1903 ever reappears,
      one of these two pins has been removed or altered; that is the first place to look.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="3.0.5" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ============================================================================================
       RESILIENCE
       Consumed by Gateway and DataServices, the two services that make outbound calls.
       ============================================================================================ -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Resilience">
    <!-- Retry and circuit breaker on the newly created network edges.

         This one addition needs justifying against the minimal change constraint, because it has
         no legacy counterpart. The justification is that AN IN PROCESS CALL CANNOT FAIL IN
         TRANSIT AND A NETWORK CALL CAN. Handling a failure mode that the decomposition itself
         creates is required BY the transition, not a behavioural improvement layered on top of
         it. Without it, the first transient network fault would surface as a defect the legacy
         framework could not possibly have had, which is a regression introduced by the refactor
         rather than a preserved behaviour.

         Pulls Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience 10.8.0 and the Polly family at 8.4.2 transitively;
         none of those needs an entry here. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience" Version="10.8.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ============================================================================================
       TESTING AND COVERAGE
       Consumed by every *.Tests project: the six shared library test projects and the four service
       test projects. CI enforces 80 percent line coverage PER IN SCOPE SERVICE, evaluated per
       service rather than repository wide so that one service's coverage cannot mask another's.
       ============================================================================================ -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Testing and coverage">
    <!-- In process host for the service level tests that sit alongside the unit tests, so that
         endpoints, authorization and status code mapping are exercised without a container. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.10" />

    <!-- Unit and service test framework.
         Two authoring consequences, both per project rather than settable here. First, an
         xunit.v3 test project MUST set OutputType to Exe. Second, these projects are HAND
         AUTHORED: dotnet new xunit3 does not exist in SDK 10.0.302, and dotnet new xunit
         scaffolds the v2 line with older test SDK and coverage packages. The hand authored shape
         was validated end to end: it restores, builds, runs, and emits Cobertura. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />

    <!-- Test adapter. Without it the standard test command discovers no xunit.v3 tests at all,
         and a zero test run reports success, which would silently void the coverage gate. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.5" />

    <!-- Test host. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.8.1" />

    <!-- Emits coverage.cobertura.xml, which is the EXACT artifact the 80 percent line coverage
         gate is measured from. Confirmed empirically by running, from a service directory,
         dotnet test with the XPlat Code Coverage collector (collect:"XPlat Code Coverage").
         Note that a bare dotnet test builds Debug even straight after a Release build, so pass
         c Release as well when release configuration coverage is wanted. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ==============================================================================================
    DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED PACKAGES. DO NOT ADD THEM.
    ==============================================================================================

    Each entry below is an "obvious" choice that is out of scope. Every one of them would be scope
    creep against the constraint that no behaviour improvement be introduced beyond what the
    technology and architecture transition strictly requires. They are recorded here, with the
    reason each was rejected, so that the decision is visible rather than rediscovered.

      Serilog.AspNetCore and the OpenTelemetry.* family
        Not in the mandated stack. The built in logging abstractions in the shared framework cover
        the requirement. The only telemetry mention in the brief is attached to the Aspire AppHost,
        which is itself rejected below.

      FluentValidation
        The five type validators ported from the legacy DataWindow service layer are PORTED LOGIC
        WITH EXACT LEGACY SEMANTICS, not a declarative rule set. Expressing them through a
        validation framework would obscure parity, which is the single property that matters most
        in this migration.

      Scalar.AspNetCore and Swashbuckle.AspNetCore
        Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi already produces the OpenAPI document. An interactive UI is
        not a requirement, and this phase creates no presentation surface at all.

      Grpc.AspNetCore.Server.Reflection
        A convenience for ad hoc command line gRPC tooling, not a requirement. The contracts are
        published as .proto files, so callers do not need server reflection to discover them.

      Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks and the URI probe health check packages
        Health check registration already ships in the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App shared framework,
        so neither the anonymous /health endpoint nor Gateway's aggregation of its three upstreams
        needs any package reference.

      Aspire.Hosting.AppHost
        The rejected orchestration alternative. Its Dockerfile builder APIs remain experimental and
        require suppressing an experimental API diagnostic, and depends_on with a service_healthy
        condition was not expressible through the generated model. That would break the
        "Gateway reports healthy only once its upstreams do" readiness gate, which is the single
        most important property of the orchestration. Hand authored Docker Compose is the one and
        only built path.

      Microsoft.SqlServer.TransactSql.ScriptDom
        Rejected as the substitute for the legacy SQL statement parser. It is SQL Server dialect
        specific, so it cannot serve the Oracle rewriter, and no BCL SQL parser exists. Since the
        acceptance criterion is BYTE EXACT clause level string output, including the legacy
        sentinel identifiers and the count wrapper alias, a focused in repository six clause model
        is both sufficient and far more verifiable. A deliberate build rather than buy decision.

      Microsoft.Data.SqlClient, any Oracle client, Npgsql, or any other database provider
        See the Data and SQLite section above. Only SQLite has evidence; the other two dialects are
        preserved as pure string paging rewriters that need no running instance and no client.

      Any third party cryptography package
        System.Security.Cryptography in the BCL covers every operation the legacy cryptographic
        surface exposes: RSA key generation, encryption, decryption, signing and verification;
        keyed hashing; the MD5 and SHA family; AES, 3DES and DES across ECB, CBC and CFB modes with
        PKCS family padding; cryptographic random generation; and GUID generation. The legacy weak
        defaults are preserved as annotated defaults in the service itself, not swapped for a
        library's safer ones.

      Any pinyin package
        Deliberately not selected. The legacy pinyin first letter lookup table exists only inside
        the closed source pfw.dll and its flag semantics are undocumented, so bit exact parity
        requires characterizing the table against the behavioural oracle rather than trusting a
        third party table that would be subtly different. This is the single genuine parity risk in
        the in scope set, and a wrong table would produce filters that return slightly wrong result
        sets, which a unit test would not catch. It is tracked as a risk, not papered over with a
        dependency.
  -->

</Project>
